    <section class="empire-glance" id="at-a-glance" aria-labelledby="glance-title">
      <div class="empire-glance-main"><h2 class="empire-glance-title" id="glance-title">At a glance</h2><ul><li>A small county in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/swabian-jura">Swabian Jura</a>, ruled from Zollern castle and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hechingen">Hechingen</a> by the Swabian branch of the Zollern dynasty.</li><li>Its Franconian cousins became <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/burgraviate-of-nuremberg">Burgraves of Nuremberg</a> and later rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brandenburg">Brandenburg</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Prussia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>.</li><li>The Catholic counts rose through <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburg</a> service, gaining <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/haigerloch">Haigerloch</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sigmaringen">Sigmaringen</a> before dividing the county in 1576.</li></ul></div>
      <aside class="empire-dyk"><p class="empire-dyk-title">Did you know?</p><p>The present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hohenzollern-castle">Hohenzollern Castle</a> is the third on its hilltop: the medieval fortress was razed by the Swabian imperial cities in 1423, and its successor was rebuilt in the 1850s–1860s.</p></aside>
    </section>

